A tiny, deliberately crude "embedding" (word overlap) is enough to show the threshold trade-off concretely without needing a real embedding model.

```python
def crude_similarity(a, b):
    """A toy stand-in for cosine similarity between real embeddings: fraction of shared words."""
    words_a, words_b = set(a.lower().split()), set(b.lower().split())
    return len(words_a & words_b) / len(words_a | words_b)

cached_prompts = {
    "how do I reset my password": "Go to Settings > Security > Reset Password.",
}

def semantic_lookup(prompt, threshold):
    best_match, best_score = None, 0.0
    for cached_prompt in cached_prompts:
        score = crude_similarity(prompt, cached_prompt)
        if score > best_score:
            best_match, best_score = cached_prompt, score
    if best_score >= threshold:
        return cached_prompts[best_match], best_score, "HIT"
    return None, best_score, "MISS (calls the LLM)"

queries = [
    "how do I reset my password",       # near-identical
    "how can I reset my account password",  # paraphrased, high overlap
    "what's the weather like today",     # unrelated
]

for threshold in [0.3, 0.7]:
    print(f"--- threshold={threshold} ---")
    for q in queries:
        result, score, outcome = semantic_lookup(q, threshold)
        print(f"  {outcome} (score={score:.2f}): {q!r}")
```

At `threshold=0.3`, the paraphrased second query (which shares several words with the cached prompt) scores well above 0.3 and correctly `HIT`s — but this looser threshold is also exactly the setting that risks a false positive on a genuinely different query if it happens to share enough words. At `threshold=0.7`, that same paraphrased query's similarity score (based on partial word overlap) likely falls short of 0.7 and becomes a `MISS`, calling the LLM even though a good cached answer existed — illustrating exactly the too-strict-barely-helps side of the trade-off the lesson describes. The unrelated third query correctly stays a `MISS` at both thresholds.
